{"id": "q0461", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 2.0, "n_chunks": 2, "n_concurrent": 2, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.0528, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": null, "query": "how do you rate strength of tornado", "transcript": "How do you rate strength of tornado?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The strength of a tornado is rated using the Enhanced Fujita Scale, or EF Scale. This scale rates tornadoes from EF0 to EF5 based on the damage they cause. For example, an EF0 is the weakest and might just break branches off trees, while an EF5 is the strongest, capable of leveling entire city blocks and tearing buildings completely off their foundations. Meteorologists use this rating to help understand how powerful a tornado was after it happens.", "answer_ms": 2192}
